      * *  H O W   T O   G E T   T H I S   T H I N G   R U N N I N G  * *

  Create a directory for the game as described in the Metro BBS docs.
Rename the file DOOR as DOOR12, or whatever door you choose.
Next, execute NewGame.Script and follow the instructions given below.

      * * * * *  H O W   T O   S E T U P   A   N E W   G A M E  * * * * *

  To setup a new game, execute the NewGame.Script.  It will delete the files
from the current game and run the SetUp.Basic program.  It will give you
several options.  SETUP GAME will let you set the start date, end date and
maximum moves per day.  Dates must be entered in MM-DD-YYYY format!  The
program does a little error checking on this, but not much.  DISTRIBUTE
OBJECTS will read the OBJECTS file and randomely distribute the objects into
the different areas.  QUIT will exit you out of the SetUp program.

  Doc.Player should be placed in your bulletin area so players will know
what the game is all about.

      * * * * * * * *  F I L E    D E S C R I P T I O N S  * * * * * * *

  Read this section only if you want to modify the game or would like to know
what all of the files are for.

  The files with .Dat extensions are the area description files.  Examples:
Main.Dat, OldWest.Dat, Space.Dat, etc.  These describe each place and the
possible moves for that place.  Also, they include the key data for entering
any sub-areas.  These files won't be described, yet.  I may write a program
for changing and adding areas in the future.

  The files with .Bad extensions are the bad guy files.  They tell where each
villian is, his name and his strength.  These can be altered, but be very
careful about the X and Y coordinates!  If you place one outside of the
defined grid, the program will bomb.  Basic description:

                             3,4\Dracula\50

3 is the X coordinate.  4 is the Y coordinate.  Dracula is the bad guy's name.
50 is his strength.  Make this value between 1 and 100.  1 is extremely
weak and 100 is exceptionally tough.

  The WEAPONS file contains a description of all weapons and their strenghts.
You can change this file if you like.  Basic description:

                             Rock\35

Rock is the weapon's name and 35 is its strength.  Remember, fighting bare-
handed is equal to a weapon strength of 30.  So, make all weapon strengths
between 31 and 100.  100 would be extremely high, though.  Hmmm, how about
a nuclear bomb?

  The OBJECTS file contains all of the objects which players can collect
for points.  You can alter or add to this file, too.  Basic description:

                              Diamond\10

Diamond is the treasure's name and 10 is its point value.  Weapon names must
appear exactly as they appear in the WEAPONS file or they won't work!
If you give something a value of 0, the Pawn Shop will NOT trade for it.
Weapons and keys have a value of 0.  This keeps someone from accidentally
trading them in.

  The NEWS file is an optional file.  You can leave daily news for all players
to read.  For example, you might want to inform everybody that you added some
new treasures to the current game or that you have changed the ending date.

  Some files are created by the SetUp program.  These include the .Obj files
and the Game.Stuff file.

  The Game.Stuff file is created by the SetUp program.  It contains:
Start Date, End Date, and Moves Per Day.  During a game, it is alright to run
SetUp and re-distribute treasures or change the End Date and Moves Per Day.

  The .Obj files are created by the SetUp program.  It takes everything in
the OBJECTS files and distributes them to all of the areas.  There is one
.Obj file per area.  Main.Obj would contain the objects found in the Main
area.  OldWest.Obj would contain the objects found in the OldWest area.
Basic description:

                           2,3\Diamond\50

2 is the X coordinate.  3 is the Y coordinate.  Diamond is the treasure's
name.  50 is its point value.  If the X or Y coordinates are beyond the limits
of the area's grid, they'll never be found.  You CAN change these files but
it should never be necessary.  They are erased and re-created when you run
SetUp and distribute the objects.

  Big-Prizes is similar but it has the valuable prizes that are placed in
locked areas.  There should be at least one valuable prize for each locked
area.

  Whenever someone plays, a file of his data is created.  If his user number
is 13, than a file named Player.13 is created.  It contains his location,
last play date, number of moves made on that day, his characteristics, the
number of objects he has, and the names of those objects.  You should never
need to edit these files, unless you won't to let someone have extra moves
or manually give or take his objects.  You could alter his characteristics,
too.  If this file does not exist, it starts the player at the beginning.
Also, this file is updated when someone enters a new area (not room), quits,
or runs out of moves.  So, if someone has played 30 moves and enters a new
area and plays 10 more moves and loses their modem connection and calls back,
the program will start them as if they had just made the 30th move.

  Top-20 contains the top twenty scores.  The game only displays 15 for a
reason.  If someone was, say, #15 and lost points by fighting than the game
needs to have a few extra scores to fill in the #15 spot.  Whenever someone
quits or runs out of moves, their score is checked against this table.

